CAT = "rsracService"; SEG = "SRV"
Q = [
("Low suction pressure, high superheat and low subcooling on a TXV system usually point to:",
 "Refrigerant undercharge", ["Refrigerant overcharge", "Dirty condenser coil", "Too much indoor airflow"],
 "Too little refrigerant starves the evaporator, raising superheat, and leaves little liquid in the condenser, lowering subcooling."),
("High subcooling with low suction pressure and high superheat suggests:",
 "Liquid line restriction", ["Refrigerant undercharge", "Overcharged system", "Non-condensables"],
 "Liquid backs up behind a restriction, raising subcooling, while the evaporator downstream starves. A plugged drier or a stuck valve is typical; a temperature drop across the part locates it."),
("A filter-drier feels noticeably colder at its outlet than its inlet. What does that indicate?",
 "Drier is restricted", ["Normal drier operation", "Refrigerant overcharge", "Clean, dry system"],
 "A pressure drop across a restriction makes liquid flash, cooling the outlet. A measurable temperature drop across a drier means it is plugging."),
("A TXV bulb strapped loosely and left uninsulated senses warm room air instead of the suction line. How does the valve respond?",
 "Overfeeds, and superheat drops", ["Closes and starves the coil", "Holds superheat normally", "Switches to heating mode"],
 "A bulb that reads warmer than the suction line builds more pressure, which drives the valve further open. The coil floods, superheat falls and liquid can reach the compressor."),
("A compressor hums but will not start, and the overload trips. Which part is checked first on a single-phase unit?",
 "Run capacitor", ["Reversing valve", "Filter-drier", "Defrost thermostat"],
 "A failed capacitor leaves the start winding without the phase shift needed to start, so the motor hums and draws locked rotor current until the overload opens."),
("Three unmarked single-phase compressor terminals read: 1 to 2, 0.6 ohm; 1 to 3, 2.4 ohms; 2 to 3, 3.0 ohms. Which terminal is common?",
 "Terminal 1", ["Terminal 2", "Terminal 3", "None, a winding is open"],
 "The highest reading, 3.0 ohms between 2 and 3, goes through both windings, so the terminal left out of that pair, 1, is common. From common, the lower reading (0.6 ohm, terminal 2) is run and the higher (terminal 3) is start."),
("A compressor's windings read infinite resistance after it trips off hot, but it restarts an hour later. What opened?",
 "Internal overload", ["Open start winding", "Failed run capacitor", "Crankcase heater"],
 "An internal thermal overload opens the common lead when the motor overheats. It reads open until the shell cools, then resets. The cause of the overheating is then found."),
("After a compressor burnout, what is added to the suction line during cleanup?",
 "Suction line drier", ["Accumulator heater", "Suction line sight glass", "Hot gas bypass"],
 "Acid and debris from a burnout circulate with the refrigerant. A suction line drier protects the new compressor and is checked for pressure drop and removed or replaced as the maker directs."),
("A compressor shell is sweating and its suction line is frosted back to the compressor. What is likely happening?",
 "Liquid floodback", ["Undercharge", "High superheat", "Low head pressure only"],
 "Very low superheat lets liquid refrigerant reach the compressor, cooling the shell and diluting the oil. An overfeeding valve or low airflow over the coil are common causes."),
("A heat pump in heating mode blows cool air, and both refrigerant lines at the outdoor unit are warm. What is suspected?",
 "Reversing valve stuck", ["Outdoor fan failed", "Dirty indoor air filter", "Defrost timer set short"],
 "A reversing valve stuck between positions bypasses hot gas to the suction side, so little heat reaches the indoor coil and both lines run warm. A temperature check on its tubes confirms it."),
("An evaporator coil keeps icing on a split air conditioner. Which cause is checked first?",
 "Airflow and filters", ["Line voltage at the panel", "Thermostat batteries", "Outdoor fan blade pitch"],
 "Low airflow from dirty filters, blocked returns or a slow blower is the most common cause of a frozen coil. Low charge is checked after airflow is confirmed."),
("Why is a wet cloth or heat paste used when brazing near a service valve?",
 "Protects the valve's internal seals", ["Lets the valve take more heat", "Adds flux to the joint", "Cools the nitrogen purge"],
 "Service valves have seals and seats that heat damages. Wrapping the valve body or using heat-blocking paste keeps it cool while the joint is brazed."),
("Before replacing a compressor on a large system, why is the system pumped down where possible?",
 "Isolates the charge", ["Cools the compressor", "Removes the oil", "Tests the reversing valve"],
 "Pumping down moves the charge into the condenser or receiver behind closed valves, so only the isolated section must be recovered."),
("A system short cycles on its high-pressure switch on a hot day. Which cause is most likely?",
 "Condenser fan failure", ["Light indoor load", "Refrigerant undercharge", "Low supply voltage"],
 "High head pressure trips the switch when the condenser cannot reject heat. A failed fan, dirty coil or recirculating air are checked first."),
("A walk-in cooler's evaporator is solidly iced after a defrost heater fails. What is the first repair step?",
 "Fully defrost the coil", ["Raise the box temperature setpoint", "Add refrigerant", "Clean the condenser"],
 "The coil must be cleared before it can be tested, then the heater, termination switch and defrost timer or board are checked to find why defrost failed."),
("A three-phase compressor's motor reads equal winding resistance but trips on overload under load. What is checked?",
 "Voltage and current per leg", ["Thermostat wiring colour", "Oil colour in the sight glass", "Refrigerant cylinder pressure"],
 "Unbalanced voltage causes much larger current imbalance and overheating. Measuring each leg's voltage and current under load finds a failed contact or supply problem."),
("A reciprocating compressor pumps poorly and its suction and discharge pressures are close together. What is suspected?",
 "Leaking valves", ["Refrigerant overcharge", "Clean condenser", "Overtightened drive belt"],
 "Damaged reed valves let gas leak back, so the compressor cannot build a pressure difference and capacity falls. A pump-down test confirms it."),
("An electronic leak detector alarms all around a unit on a windy rooftop. What helps pinpoint the leak?",
 "Shield the area from wind", ["Turn the sensitivity right up", "Move the probe faster", "Add refrigerant first"],
 "Wind spreads leaking vapour over the whole unit, so the detector alarms everywhere. Sheltering the area lets the vapour concentrate at the leak; higher sensitivity alarms even more widely."),
("A repaired leak has been brazed. What is done before the system is recharged?",
 "Test, evacuate, recharge", ["Recharge and watch the gauges", "Add refrigerant to the old charge", "Run it empty to dry it"],
 "A repair is confirmed with a pressure or leak test, then the system is evacuated and charged. Halocarbon rules, federal and provincial, require a leak to be repaired before recharging."),
("A system on a high-glide blend lost most of its charge through a vapour leak. How is it recharged?",
 "Recover and weigh in fresh", ["Top off with vapour", "Add pure R-32 to the blend", "Charge until the sight glass clears"],
 "A vapour leak can shift a blend's composition. Recovering what is left and charging a fresh weighed charge restores the blend's intended mix."),
]
